<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Sultanate of Mayotte</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1841 CE (approx.) · capital Tsingoni</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Shirazi sultanate of Mayotte, whose last ruler, the exiled Sakalava king Andriantsoly, sold the island to France in 1841.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>A Shirazi Muslim dynasty established itself on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-mayotte-and-dependencies">Mayotte</a> (Maore) around 1500, linked by marriage and trade to the other Comorian islands. The sultans ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsingoni">Tsingoni</a>, where the Friday mosque, dated by inscription to 1538, is among the oldest in the region.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sultanate was a small maritime state trading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and slaves with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> coast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> and passing European ships. Its rulers were often drawn into the wars of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-anjouan">Sultanate of Anjouan</a>, which claimed overlordship, and the island suffered heavy Malagasy slave raids in the late 18th century. The capital later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzaoudzi">Dzaoudzi</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1832 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andriantsoly">Andriantsoly</a>, the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakalava">Sakalava</a> king of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/boina">Boina</a> driven out by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/merina-kingdom">Merina Kingdom</a>, seized <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-mayotte-and-dependencies">Mayotte</a>. Seeking protection, he sold the island to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1841. Mayotte remained French and is now an overseas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="sultanate, 1500 CE – 1841 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">sultanate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:89.55%;width:6.79%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1841 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tsingoni</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Andriantsoly</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Shimaore, Kibushi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Maore, Maute</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1670&amp;focus=sultanate-of-mayotte" aria-label="Sultanate of Mayotte on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5918 2654 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sultanate of Mayotte lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6238 2910L6220 2887M6238 2910L6243 2910"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6220 2887h0M6243 2910h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6238 2910h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6238 2910h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tsingoni. <a href="/atlas?year=1670&amp;focus=sultanate-of-mayotte">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
